# Citation File Format 1.2.0 — https://citation-file-format.github.io
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS
#     Every citation this project invited resolved to lapledger.org, which is
#     one domain registration. A reader who wants to cite the database — a
#     journalist, a Wikipedia editor arguing WP:RS, anyone filing a figure
#     they may have to defend later — had no citation to copy and nothing
#     naming the work apart from the page they were on. GitHub renders a
#     "Cite this repository" button from this file, in BibTeX and APA.
#
#     `version` below is VERSION in build.py. It is not copied by hand and
#     left to rot: tests/test_conventions.py fails the build when the two
#     drift, the same way a README figure that disagrees with the database
#     does. Bumping VERSION for a release means bumping it here, and the
#     comment beside VERSION says so.
#
# WHAT THIS FILE DELIBERATELY DOES NOT SAY
#     **No `license:`.** CFF's licence list is OR, not AND: "when there are
#     multiple licenses, it is assumed their relationship is OR". So naming
#     MIT, CC-BY-SA-4.0 and ODbL-1.0 together would not describe a repository
#     holding three kinds of thing — it would offer every one of them under
#     any one of the three, MIT included. This project cannot offer the
#     Wikipedia-derived data or the OpenStreetMap centrelines in
#     f1-geometry.db under MIT; that is a grant of rights it does not hold,
#     and it would dissolve the two share-alike obligations the split
#     between f1.db and f1-geometry.db exists to keep. CFF cannot express
#     the split, so this file does not pretend to: the terms are in LICENSE,
#     LICENSE-DATA and ATTRIBUTION.md, and a single id here would be a
#     licence position rather than a restatement — PD-41 (#481) is open on
#     what that position is.
#
#     **No `date-released`.** BUILT moves on a harvest refresh and VERSION
#     only on a release, so binding a release date to BUILT would sooner or
#     later date v2.24 to a day on which no v2.24 was released, and no
#     in-tree record says when a tag was published. A citation that cannot
#     be checked is one this file does not make.
#
#     **No `doi:`.** Nobody has minted one. Zenodo's GitHub integration is
#     an account switch a maintainer flips, not something a build can do,
#     and a DOI written down before it exists is a citation that resolves to
#     nothing. SD-31 (#555) holds it.

cff-version: 1.2.0
message: If you use this database, please cite it as below.
title: "Lap Ledger: a verified SQLite database of Formula One"
type: dataset
authors:
  - family-names: Farley
    given-names: Alex
repository-code: https://github.com/Alex-Farley/formula-1-data
url: https://lapledger.org
abstract: >-
  A normalised SQLite database of Formula One — championships, races,
  finishing orders, drivers, constructors, circuits, chassis and engines —
  built from public sources by a dependency-free Python build and gated by
  cross-checks that refuse a fact they cannot reconcile. The database is a
  pure function of its sources: it rebuilds byte-for-byte, so a published
  digest identifies exactly one file. Where two sources disagree the
  difference is published rather than reconciled, and where nobody has
  established a fact it is recorded as a gap instead of being invented.
  Race timing is deliberately absent — no licence on offer permits passing
  it on — which is about race timing rather than lap times, and about the
  licences available rather than about what anyone may lawfully do. The
  terms each part is offered under are in LICENSE, LICENSE-DATA and
  ATTRIBUTION.md; the circuit centrelines are a separate file under a
  separate licence.
keywords:
  - Formula One
  - motorsport
  - open data
  - SQLite
  - sports statistics
version: "2.24"
